 to ask: which polyominoes tile the plane?\n\n## The Translation Criterion\n\nIn the most restricted sense, we can ask if a polyomino tiles the plane by translation only—that is, by sliding copies of itself without rotating or flipping. Whether a polyomino can do this is determined by the translation criterion:\n\n> A polyomino satisfies the Translation Criterion if its border can be divided into six segments A, B, C, D, E, F such that the pairs A-D, B-E, and C-F are translations of each other.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUndecidability of Translational Tiling of the Plane with Orthogonally Convex Polyominoes · Pith (https://pith.science/paper/2506.12726)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; The paper proves that no algorithm can decide whether an arbitrary set of seven orthogonally convex polyominoes tiles the plane by translation. ... The load-bearing object is the level-3 square of order $(13,22)$, a bundle of 22 by 22 level-2 squares, each an order-13 diamond-shaped 'square simulator' that carries either a dent or a bump on its northwest or southeast side. ...   * Any Wang tile set that admits only nonperiodic tilings would yield, through this construction, a set of seven orthogonally convex polyominoes that also tiles only nonperiodically, since the polyomino tiling exists exactly when the Wang tiling does.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroduction ▪ The Labyrinth of Polyominoes (https://minos.tessera.li/intro)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Packing Fitting a set of polyominoes tightly in some container.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParticle-Based Assembly Using Precise Global Control | Algorithmica | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00453-022-00992-2)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.